    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1958-p11.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Factoring gives \\[ (1-x)\\sqrt{5-x}=0. \\] Thus either \\(x=1\\) or \\(5-x=0,\\) giving \\(x=5.\\) Both values lie in the domain \\(x\\le5\\) and satisfy the original equation.\n\nTherefore, there are two roots, and the correct answer is C.",
      "hints": [
        "Move both sides to one side and factor the common radical",
        "Check separately when the radical is zero and when its remaining factor is zero"
      ],
